---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import BulkBar from "../../components/BulkBar.astro";
import { page, results } from "../../lib/results";

const { totals, bulk, edges, agreement } = results;
const count = (value: number) => value.toLocaleString("en-GB");
const buildNames: Record<string, string> = {
  macos: "macOS",
  "linux-glibc-arm64": "Linux with glibc on ARM",
  "linux-musl-arm64": "Linux with musl on ARM",
  "linux-glibc-x86": "Linux with glibc on x86",
  "linux-musl-x86": "Linux with musl on x86",
};
const builds = (agreement?.builds ?? []).map((name: string) => buildNames[name] ?? name);
const attacks = totals.policy_refused + totals.policy_escaped;
const harmless = totals.policy_allowed + totals.policy_over_refused;
const day = (seconds: number) =>
  new Date(seconds * 1000).toLocaleDateString("en-GB", { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric", timeZone: "UTC" });
---

<Base title="Policy tests" description="How termite's policy is tested with no AI model involved." current="policy">
  <h1>Policy tests</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    These tests use no AI model. They send commands straight to termite's policy, to check
    termite itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    The policy is a fixed set of rules in termite's code. It allows a command only if it can prove
    that the command only reads. It does not depend on a model, so it still works when a model
    writes something dangerous. The same commands always give the same result.
  </p>
  <p>
    No command is run in these tests. The policy reads each command as text and gives a result.
  </p>

  <h2>Known attacks</h2>
  <p>
    I wrote a fixed list of {attacks + harmless} commands. {attacks} are known attacks that the
    policy must block, and {harmless} are harmless commands that it must allow.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Commands</th><th scope="col" class="number">Correct</th><th scope="col" class="number">Wrong</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Known attacks, which must be blocked</th><td class="number">{totals.policy_refused} of {attacks}</td><td class="number">{totals.policy_escaped}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Harmless commands, which must be allowed</th><td class="number">{totals.policy_allowed} of {harmless}</td><td class="number">{totals.policy_over_refused}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="note">
    Last run on {day(results.started)}, on the Local Sandbox on Mac, with code version {results.commit}.
  </p>
  <p>
    This test shows that the policy stops the attacks on this list. It cannot show that the policy
    stops an attack that nobody has thought of yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Commands from public datasets</h2>
  <p>
    I also sent every command in two public datasets through the policy:{" "}
    {bulk.total.toLocaleString("en-GB")} commands, after duplicates were removed. Nothing was left
    out because of its size or shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    These datasets were made to train models, and most of their commands are unverified. They are
    not a record of commands that people ran. Some of the commands contain typing errors.
  </p>
  <BulkBar />
  <p class="note">
    Last run on {day(bulk.started)}, on the Local Sandbox on Mac, with code version {bulk.commit}.
  </p>
  <p>
    This test shows what the policy allows across many kinds of command. A blocked command is one
    that the policy could not prove only reads. Many of these commands do write, so this figure does
    not show how much harmless work the policy blocks.
  </p>

  {edges && (
    <>
      <h2>Edge tests</h2>
      <p>
        A limit is a common place for a check to fail. termite refuses a command longer than 500
        characters, so this test makes {count(edges.copies)} padded copies of the fixed commands
        above. Their lengths are just under, at and just over the limit, and then far over it. Each
        command is padded in four ways: with spaces, a long argument, a comment and multi-byte
        characters.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col" class="number">Copies</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Blocked, as they must be</th><td class="number">{count(edges.blocked)}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Allowed, and they should have been blocked</th><td class="number">{count(edges.escaped)}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Harmless, and still allowed</th><td class="number">{count(edges.kept)}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Harmless, and blocked after padding</th><td class="number">{count(edges.changed)}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="note">
        Last run on {day(edges.started)}, on the Local Sandbox on Mac, with code version {edges.commit}.
      </p>
      <p>
        A harmless command can be blocked after padding because the padding changes it. An extra
        argument can turn a command that only reads into one that writes. This result is stricter,
        so it is safe.
      </p>
      <p>
        The normal test suite also sends broken replies from a fake model server, such as an empty
        reply, a missing score or a score outside the allowed range. None of them can make a command
        count as safe.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  {agreement && (
    <>
      <h2>Build agreement</h2>
      <p>
        The same program can behave differently when it is built for a different system. This test
        sends the same {count(agreement.commands)} commands to {builds.length} builds of termite, and
        compares the results. Each command gets {agreement.checks} checks: one on the text alone, and
        one in each of {agreement.checks - 1} made-up folders.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>{count(agreement.disagreements)}</strong> {agreement.disagreements === 1 ? "command gave" : "commands gave"} a
        different result on a different build.
      </p>
      <p>Builds: {builds.join("; ")}.</p>
      <p class="note">
        Last run on {day(agreement.started)}, with code version {agreement.commit}. The Linux builds ran in
        containers with no network.
      </p>
      <p>
        If all the builds agree, it does not prove that they are right, because they can all be wrong
        in the same way. Other systems may also give different results.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <p>
    <a href={page("docs/tests/#tests-that-use-no-model")}>Read how these tests fit with the model tests</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
